      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{150}^{∘})=$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={150}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{150}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
