      "problem": "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
